          <trans-unit id="guide.documentation" resname="guide.documentation">
        <source>Documentation</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.sitemaps.title" resname="guide.sitemaps.title">
        <source>How sitemap snapshots work</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.sitemaps.purpose" resname="guide.sitemaps.purpose">
        <source>A snapshot stores the sitemaps of every language of a site at one point in time, raw XML included. It is the URL list every check works with, and it stays available after the site itself has changed.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.sitemaps.step1" resname="guide.sitemaps.step1">
        <source>Choose a base URL of a configured site and find its sitemaps. The environment is suggested from the site configuration.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.sitemaps.step2" resname="guide.sitemaps.step2">
        <source>Select the languages, give the snapshot a label if you like, and import it.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.sitemaps.step3" resname="guide.sitemaps.step3">
        <source>Lock every snapshot you must not lose, e.g. the live state before a relaunch: the lock icon protects it from deletion, also by the cleanup command.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.sitemaps.note" resname="guide.sitemaps.note">
        <source>URLs outside the configured sites and scheduled imports need the command line: websitecheck:importsitemaps. Before the database is replaced, save the snapshots you need as files below.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.status.title" resname="guide.status.title">
        <source>How the status check works</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.status.purpose" resname="guide.status.purpose">
        <source>Checks whether every URL of a sitemap snapshot works: the HTTP status and TYPO3 error pages per URL. The link check also follows the plugin links on those pages, which no sitemap lists, and reports links whose arguments have no effect.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.status.step1" resname="guide.status.step1">
        <source>Import a sitemap snapshot of the environment to check in the Sitemaps module.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.status.step2" resname="guide.status.step2">
        <source>Compose the check below and run the command on the command line. Every URL is requested, which can take a while.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.status.step3" resname="guide.status.step3">
        <source>Reload this page and review the results: filter by environment and problems, mark findings as reviewed and add a note where needed.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.status.note" resname="guide.status.note">
        <source>Moved URLs and redirects are the job of the migration check: it compares two environments page by page.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.migration.title" resname="guide.migration.title">
        <source>How the migration check works</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.migration.purpose" resname="guide.migration.purpose">
        <source>Checks whether the URLs search engines know from the old site still lead to the same page or record on the new one — directly or through a redirect.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.migration.step1" resname="guide.migration.step1">
        <source>Before the switch: import the sitemaps of the live site and lock the snapshot.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.migration.step2" resname="guide.migration.step2">
        <source>Import a snapshot of the new site, e.g. staging, compose the check below and run it. Repeat after every change to the new site.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.migration.step3" resname="guide.migration.step3">
        <source>Review the results: missing redirects per page and record, with a suggested target where one can be derived.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.migration.step4" resname="guide.migration.step4">
        <source>After the switch: import a snapshot of the new live site and reuse the reference results of the last run — the old site no longer answers. Export the last run beforehand if the database gets replaced.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="guide.migration.note" resname="guide.migration.note">
        <source>Both sites have to render the page and record markers described in the documentation; without them pages cannot be compared.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.statusHost.title" resname="help.statusHost.title">
        <source>Other host</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.statusHost" resname="help.statusHost">
        <source>Requests the paths of the snapshot on another host. Example: the snapshot was taken on www.example.com, the host is staging.example.com — you see before the switch whether every URL search engines know answers on the new system. Or the other way round: a sitemap provider only exists on staging while the pages are live already. Redirects and content are compared by the migration check.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.statusEnvironment.title" resname="help.statusEnvironment.title">
        <source>Environment label</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.statusEnvironment" resname="help.statusEnvironment">
        <source>Results are stored per environment label and URL; a new run with the same label replaces them. Use one label per environment and check, e.g. "staging" for the status check and "staging-links" for the link check of the same site.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.statusSamples.title" resname="help.statusSamples.title">
        <source>Links per shape</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.statusSamples" resname="help.statusSamples">
        <source>A shape is the path of a link plus the names of its arguments. /news/detail?tx_news_pi1[news]=12 and …[news]=13 share one shape, so a list of a hundred news items is checked with this many links, not a hundred.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.migrationReference.title" resname="help.migrationReference.title">
        <source>Reference snapshot</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.migrationReference" resname="help.migrationReference">
        <source>The URLs of the old site as search engines know them. Take the locked live snapshot from before the switch — a newer live snapshot already lists the URLs of the new site.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.migrationReferenceRun.title" resname="help.migrationReferenceRun.title">
        <source>Reference results</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.migrationReferenceRun" resname="help.migrationReferenceRun">
        <source>Before the switch, both sites answer and every run requests both. After it, www.example.com shows the new site: a run named "relaunch" from before the switch still knows how every old URL answered, and a run "after-relaunch" reuses those rows and only requests the new site.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.migrationLabels.title" resname="help.migrationLabels.title">
        <source>Environment labels</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.migrationLabels" resname="help.migrationLabels">
        <source>They name the two sides in the results, e.g. "live" and "staging". After the switch both are live: "live-before" and "live-after" keep them apart.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.sitemapsEnvironment.title" resname="help.sitemapsEnvironment.title">
        <source>Environment</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.sitemapsEnvironment" resname="help.sitemapsEnvironment">
        <source>Says where the snapshot comes from. The migration check suggests the locked live snapshot as reference and the newest staging, development or local one as target — so tag live snapshots as live.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.sitemapsLabel.title" resname="help.sitemapsLabel.title">
        <source>Label</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="help.sitemapsLabel" resname="help.sitemapsLabel">
        <source>The name the checks use to find the snapshot, e.g. "live-before-relaunch". It has to be unique. Left empty, it is the host and the time, e.g. "www.example.com 2026-01-31 14:05".</source>
      </trans-unit>
          <trans-unit id="archive.title" resname="archive.title">
        <source>Saved files</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="archive.help" resname="archive.help">
        <source>A database that is replaced — e.g. by a fresh import of the live database — takes every snapshot and every stored check result with it. A saved file brings them back, and it moves them to another installation, e.g. from staging to the new live system. The files are the same as those of websitecheck:exportsnapshots.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="archive.intro" resname="archive.intro">
        <source>Snapshots and migration check runs saved to %1$s. Reading a file in skips what is already here; a label used by another record stops it. Keep the directory out of version control.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="archive.empty" resname="archive.empty">
        <source>No saved files yet. Save a snapshot with the button in its header, or a migration check run in the migration check module.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="archive.disabled" resname="archive.disabled">
        <source>Saving to files is switched off. Set an archive directory in Settings → Extension Configuration → ot_websitecheck, e.g. data/websitecheck, to save snapshots here before the database is replaced.</source>
      </trans-unit>
